fix(reviewer): the echoed prompt is not the engine's answer (ASK-287)

Codex found this reviewing PR #86 round 2 (major, pr-review-agent.sh:638) while
the suite it shipped with was green at 206/206.

`run_engine` captures the WHOLE `codex exec` transcript (`> "$2" 2>&1`), and the
transcript replays the prompt before the model runs. The reviewer's prompt ENDS
by showing the exact block it wants back:

    FINDINGS: / severity|one-sentence claim|file:line / END FINDINGS

so every codex artifact carries a syntactically complete findings block that the
model did not write. Measured on the real out-of-credits shape:

    has_complete_findings_block: TRUE
    verdict_from_findings:       'APPROVE'

on a run where the engine emitted zero tokens. That is worse than the defect this
issue opened on. Round 1 made a complete FINDINGS block the FIRST test precisely
so no widening of the outage predicate could route a parseable review into the
fallback -- and the echoed template made every outage "parseable", so the outage
branch was unreachable and an unreviewed PR derived a green required check.

Root cause is again the fixture, one level down. Round 1 grounded the banner and
the hook chatter from a live capture but retyped the echoed prompt as the single
line `Review this PR.`. A one-line stand-in carries no block, so the fixture
could not see the block the real prompt puts in every transcript.

THE CUT IS THE ASSISTANT-TURN MARKER, the same structural property of the
producer the outage predicate already reads. `codex` alone on a line is printed
before the model's first token: above it is the harness talking to itself,
below it is the engine's own output.

    turn marker present -> parse ONLY what follows the first one
    turn marker absent  -> the model never spoke, so there is no content

It lands in `findings_block` -- the ONE reader -- not in pr-review-agent.sh's
branch order. A caller-side ordering fix leaves the lib handing APPROVE to the
next consumer that asks, which is the "safety lived in one caller instead of in
the reader" defect the lib's own comments were written about. `claude -p` writes
no banner and no echo, so its reviews have no transcript to strip and are read
whole; the transcript test is anchored to the banner in the first 10 lines, where
the producer writes it (measured: line 2).

The garbage rule does NOT move. A transcript WITH a turn marker and an unclosed
block still reads as unusable: UNSTATED, no fallback, nothing invented.

Tests:
  - The preamble fixture now SLICES the echoed template out of pr-review-agent.sh
    at test time, so it cannot drift from the prompt it imitates. Retyping it is
    what hid this defect for a round.
  - Lib-level: a transcript with no assistant turn derives NOTHING; a transcript
    WITH one still derives from the model's own block, not the echo above it.
  - Mutation: strip the two transcript lines out of findings_block and the
    out-of-credits artifact derives APPROVE again, so the assertions are carried
    by the fix and not by something else.

Observed RED on Q4D before the fix (the exact finding, reproduced), then:
  test-severity-floor.sh        209/209
  test-findings-block-reader.sh   9/9
  test-review-tree-guard.sh      22/22
  bash -n on lib + suite         PARSE OK

